-- title:
--   Derivative ideal composition and minimal-prime persistence
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $R$ be commutative rings, with $R$ a $K$-algebra, and let $D:R\to R$ be a $K$-derivation. For an ideal $I\subseteq R$ and $n\in\mathbb N$, define
--
--   $$P_n(I)=\bigl(D^k f:f\in I,\ 0\le k\le n\bigr),$$
--
--   the ideal generated by all such derivatives, where $D^0$ is the identity.
--
--   Then $P_0(I)=I$ and, for all natural numbers $m,n$,
--
--   $$P_n(P_m(I))=P_{m+n}(I).$$
--
--   Moreover, if $\mathfrak p$ is a minimal prime over $P_m(I)$, then
--
--   $$
--   \mathfrak p\text{ is a minimal prime over }P_{m+n}(I)
--   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
--   D^k f\in\mathfrak p\quad(f\in P_m(I),\ 0\le k\le n).
--   $$
--
--   Thus persistence of a minimal prime between these two derivative stages is exactly finite-order differential contact on the earlier ideal. Neither Noetherianity nor characteristic zero is required for this algebraic assertion.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The composition identity is the ordinary one-derivation affine algebra underlying the operator-composition step in [Philippon (1986), Proposition 4.3, pp. 373–374](https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.2060.pdf). The complete theorem does not formalize the full projective translation and component-saturation statement. It supplies a bridge to the component-selection argument; existence of a suitable persistent component and the uniform total degree bound remain Open in the A.1 application.
-- source:
--   Philippon (1986), Lemmes de zeros dans les groupes algebriques commutatifs, Bull. Soc. Math. France 114, 355-383. Proposition 4.3, pp. 373-374: composition of derivative operators on ideals; section 5, pp. 380-382: the component-selection argument. https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.2060.pdf. Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, Theorem A.2. https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. One-derivation affine algebraic composition lemma, with an exact minimal-prime persistence/contact equivalence. This is not the full projective translation and component-saturation statement of Proposition 4.3. Selecting a persistent component and proving the uniform global degree bound remain open.

import Definitions.Def_WeierstrassEllipticZeta_ChartProlongationData

open TranscendenceTheory

theorem TranscendenceTheory.differential_prolongation_component_persistence
    (K R : Type*) [CommRing K] [CommRing R] [Algebra K R]
    (D : Derivation K R R) (I : Ideal R) :
    differentialProlongation D I 0 = I ∧
    (∀ m n : ℕ, differentialProlongation D (differentialProlongation D I m) n =
      differentialProlongation D I (m + n)) ∧
    ∀ (m n : ℕ) (p : Ideal R), p ∈ (differentialProlongation D I m).minimalPrimes →
      (p ∈ (differentialProlongation D I (m + n)).minimalPrimes ↔
        ∀ f ∈ differentialProlongation D I m, ∀ k ≤ n, (D^[k]) f ∈ p) := by sorry
